raw.opening_B:
{
"explanation": "The Practice Varen stamp is a parity-style rule over the three features. Treat circle, dot, and left as 0, and square, line, and right as 1. An input earns PASS when the sum of its three bits is odd, meaning exactly one or all three of its features are on the 'second' side. That yields four PASSes: Q1 (all zeros) is the one exception to nothing here, since the sum there is even, so it FAILs. The PASSes are Q4 (circle, line, right: sum 2, even) — checking this carefully, the odd-sum inputs are Q2, Q3, Q5, and Q8. Q2 has only the box on the second side, Q3 only the line, Q5 only the square, and Q8 has all three. So the stamp passes those four and fails Q1, Q4, Q6, and Q7, with no exceptions. Changing any single feature always flips the outcome.",
"checks": {
"Q4": "FAIL",
"Q5": "PASS",
"Q8": "PASS"
}
}

